Simple subungual hematoma from hammer strike to thumb
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Visible subungual blood collection, Intense throbbing digital pain, Direct crush impact mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe throbbing thumb pain following a crush injury, requiring urgent assessment for nail bed disruption and tuft fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, high-velocity crush injury to the thumb tip resulting in severe, unremitting throbbing pain. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to evaluate for an open tuft fracture, assess the integrity of the germinal matrix, and perform immediate nail trephination to relieve acute compartment pressure and pain before morning clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I missed the nail and hit my thumb with the hammer; it is throbbing uncontrollably and turning black underneath.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a direct, high-velocity crush impact to the digit tip occurring 45 minutes ago. This resulted in an immediate, intense, localized throbbing pain isolated strictly to the distal phalanx and nail bed. The patient notes a rapidly expanding, visible collection of dark red blood confined securely beneath the intact nail plate. The extreme point tenderness is exacerbated by even light touch or minor pressure applied directly to the nail surface.
Functional Status: Patient is vigorously shaking the hand, unable to use the thumb for any grasp due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: DIY tools remain out; patient advised to clear them.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, in acute localized distress, clutching the affected hand and elevating it.
Physical Exam: Digit examination reveals a visible subungual blood collection covering approximately 60% of the nail bed. There is intense, throbbing digital pain. The nail plate remains firmly attached to the underlying nail bed and proximal eponychial fold. Swelling is confined strictly to the fingertip pulp and periungual tissues. Active flexion and extension of the terminal interphalangeal joint are intact. Normal distal capillary refill is noted in the adjacent uninjured fingers. There is an absence of a jagged deep laceration extending past the cuticle into the germinal matrix.
Environmental Observations: Hammer and nails safely placed aside.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple subungual hematoma to thumb - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Open distal tuft fracture - Ruled out due to: Intact nail plate and skin without an open laceration connecting to the bony tuft.

Nail bed laceration requiring repair - Ruled out due to: Secure, non-displaced nail plate without structural avulsion.

Extensor tendon rupture (Mallet thumb) - Ruled out due to: Intact, strong active extension at the interphalangeal joint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The mechanism of a direct hammer strike to the digit creates a closed compartment crush injury, rapidly building hydrostatic pressure beneath the rigid nail plate. Bedside examination confirmed an isolated subungual hematoma with an intact nail plate and active tendon mechanics. Urgent after-hours assessment and intervention were absolutely critical to perform immediate bedside nail trephination, instantly releasing the trapped blood. This urgent decompression immediately resolved the agonizing neuropathic pain and avoided an unnecessary, painful emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed sterile bedside nail trephination using a heated electrocautery device/sterile large-bore needle, yielding immediate egress of dark blood and instant, profound pain relief. Dressed with a simple dry, non-adherent dressing. Advised a non-urgent plain radiograph if bony pain persists, though management would remain conservative.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the finger elevated and wrapped in a simple dry dressing. The trephination hole allows residual blood to drain, directly preventing pressure from rebuilding and ensuring the throbbing pain does not return overnight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular finger bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to ensure no secondary infection has occurred.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the local GP or Emergency Department if the fingertip becomes increasingly hot, red, excessively swollen, or if thick yellow/green pus begins oozing from the small drainage hole.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Underlying hairline tuft fractures are common with this mechanism but are managed conservatively exactly as done today; the nail acts as a natural splint.
Clinical Course: The acute throbbing has been relieved. The nail plate will likely detach completely and fall off painlessly in 2 to 4 weeks, with a new nail already growing beneath it.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if the severe throbbing returns, or if the red skin surrounding the nail begins to spread rapidly down the finger.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Burning a tiny hole through the dead nail plate instantly releases the trapped, pressurized blood pressing on the highly sensitive nerve endings.
Actionable Steps: Do not submerge the finger in dirty water (e.g., washing dishes). Expect small amounts of dark blood to ooze from the hole for 24 hours; this is normal and prevents pressure buildup. Do not try to pry the nail off.

